---
/**
 * The report opening.
 *
 * Ordered as the argument runs rather than as a landing page: what the number is, why it cannot
 * be read alone, the grid, the claim, the inversion, and where the rest of the document is.
 *
 * Pacing here has three instruments and no others, which is the point of this prototype. The
 * measure — a figure inside the reading column, out into the margin, or across the whole sheet.
 * Density — prose, then a 720-cell grid, then a table, then prose again. And typographic scale,
 * from a 76px chapter opening to an 11px source note. There is no photography, no change of
 * ground and no colour-as-subject to reach for.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import CoverageGrid from '../components/CoverageGrid.astro';
import Scatter from '../components/Scatter.astro';
import { OVERALL, R, HEADLINE_INVERSION, byCoverage, num, pct } from '../lib/stats';
import { MONTHS } from '../lib/generate';

const h = HEADLINE_INVERSION;
---
<Base
  title="The Monitoring Gap 2026 — Stell"
  description="Reported storm-overflow spills track how much of the monitoring estate was working, not how much sewage reached the river. Twelve catchments, sixty months, simulated."
>
  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">Report 04 · {OVERALL.months} months · {OVERALL.catchments} catchments</p>
        <h1 class="chapter">You cannot count what nobody was watching.</h1>
        <p class="stand">
          Storm overflows are fitted with monitors that record when a discharge starts and stops.
          The counts those monitors produce are the figures everybody quotes. They are also, and
          only, counts of what was seen.
        </p>
        <div class="prose prose--drop">
          <p>
            A monitor that is offline does not record a zero. It records nothing, and nothing is
            indistinguishable from a quiet outfall by the time the figures are added up. Monitor
            availability is published. It is published separately from the spill counts, in a
            different file, and almost nobody joins the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            This report joins them across {OVERALL.catchments} catchments and {OVERALL.months} months.
            Mean coverage over the period was <span class="thread">{pct(OVERALL.meanCoverage, 1)}</span>.
            In the worst catchment it was {pct(OVERALL.worstCoverage, 1)}; in the worst single month
            anywhere in the study, {OVERALL.worstMonth.s.catchment.name} had
            {pct(OVERALL.worstMonth.p.coverage)} of its estate reporting.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Simulated</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            Every catchment and every figure here is generated. The catchments are invented. The
            mechanism is real and the generator is <a class="u" href="/method">published</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Estate covered</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{num(OVERALL.overflows)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">storm overflows, serving {num(Math.round(OVERALL.population / 1000))}k people</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Mean coverage</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{pct(OVERALL.meanCoverage, 1)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">of monitors reporting, across the whole period</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc doc--wide">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">Figure</p>
        <h2 class="head2">Sixty months of watchfulness, and where it lapsed.</h2>
        <Figure
          n={1}
          width="full"
          title="Monitor coverage by catchment and month, 2021–2025. Each cell is one catchment-month; darker is more of the estate reporting."
          source="Simulated. Generator, seed and parameters on the method page."
          note="Read across for a history and down for a comparison. The pale bands are not low numbers in a column — they are months in which most of a catchment's outfalls were not being watched at all."
        >
          <CoverageGrid />
        </Figure>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Best covered</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            {byCoverage[byCoverage.length - 1].catchment.name}, {pct(byCoverage[byCoverage.length - 1].totals.coverage, 1)}
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Worst covered</p>
          <p class="margin__v">{byCoverage[0].catchment.name}, {pct(byCoverage[0].totals.coverage, 1)}</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Reading the grid</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            Hover or tab a cell for that month's coverage, its reported spills and — because this
            is a simulation — the discharges that actually occurred.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc doc--wide">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">The finding</p>
        <h2 class="head2">Published figures track monitoring, not pollution.</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Reported spills per overflow correlate with coverage at{' '}
            <span class="thread">r = {R.reportedVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>. The underlying rate —
            the discharges the simulation actually produced — does not correlate with coverage at
            all, at r = {R.latentVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those two numbers together are the whole report. The second is the control: the two
            inputs were drawn independently, so if it had a slope the finding would be an artefact
            of how the data was made rather than of how it was observed. It is plotted on the same
            axes below, in outline, so a reader can check that rather than take our word.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Figure
          n={2}
          width="full"
          title="Reported spills per overflow against monitor coverage. Filled points are what would be published; outlined points are the simulated underlying rate on the same axes."
          source="Simulated. Least-squares fit through the reported points."
          note={`The dashed line is the fit through the reported points. The outlined cloud has no comparable slope, which is what a control is for. ${h.dirtier.catchment.name} and ${h.cleaner.catchment.name} are marked; they are the subject of finding 3.`}
        >
          <Scatter />
        </Figure>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Reported ~ coverage</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{R.reportedVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">what the published counts measure</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Underlying ~ coverage</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{R.latentVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">the control. Near zero, as it must be</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">See also</p>
          <p class="margin__v"><a class="u" href="/method">How the dataset is generated, and what that does and does not license</a></p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">The inversion</p>
        <h2 class="head2">
          {h.dirtier.catchment.name} discharges more than {h.cleaner.catchment.name} and reports less.
        </h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            {h.dirtier.catchment.name} has an underlying rate of
            {h.dirtier.totals.latentPerOverflow.toFixed(1)} discharges per overflow per year.
            {h.cleaner.catchment.name} has {h.cleaner.totals.latentPerOverflow.toFixed(1)} — a little
            over half. On the figures that would be published,{' '}
            <span class="thread">{h.dirtier.catchment.name} reports {h.dirtier.totals.perOverflow.toFixed(1)}{' '}
            and {h.cleaner.catchment.name} reports {h.cleaner.totals.perOverflow.toFixed(1)}</span>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The difference between them is not the rivers. It is that
            {h.cleaner.catchment.name} had {pct(h.cleaner.totals.coverage)} of its monitors working
            and {h.dirtier.catchment.name} had {pct(h.dirtier.totals.coverage)}.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is not the only such pair.{' '}
            <a class="u" href="/findings#f3">There are eighteen inversions in the study</a>, of which
            this is the widest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <table class="tbl">
          <caption class="vh">The inversion pair compared</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Catchment</th><th scope="col" class="n">Underlying</th><th scope="col" class="n">Reported</th><th scope="col" class="n">Coverage</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[h.dirtier, h.cleaner].map((series) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><a href={`/catchments/${series.catchment.slug}`}>{series.catchment.name}</a></th>
                <td class="n">{series.totals.latentPerOverflow.toFixed(1)}</td>
                <td class="n">{series.totals.perOverflow.toFixed(1)}</td>
                <td class="n">{pct(series.totals.coverage)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Units</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            Discharges per storm overflow per year, averaged over the {MONTHS}-month period.
            Per-overflow rather than absolute, so a large estate is not automatically the worst.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Both pages</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            <a class="u" href={`/catchments/${h.dirtier.catchment.slug}`}>{h.dirtier.catchment.name}</a> ·
            <a class="u" href={`/catchments/${h.cleaner.catchment.slug}`}>{h.cleaner.catchment.name}</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">The rest of the report</p>
        <h2 class="head2">Four chapters, and the data underneath them.</h2>
        <ol class="toc">
          <li class="toc__row">
            <a href="/findings"><span class="toc__n">01</span><span class="toc__t">Findings</span>
            <span class="toc__d">Five numbered findings, each with the figure it rests on. Written to be cited.</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="toc__row">
            <a href="/catchments"><span class="toc__n">02</span><span class="toc__t">Catchments</span>
            <span class="toc__d">All twelve, with a page each: the monthly series, the coverage history, and what its published figures would and would not support.</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="toc__row">
            <a href="/method"><span class="toc__n">03</span><span class="toc__t">Method</span>
            <span class="toc__d">The generator, the seed, the parameters, the controls, and a list of the things this report is not entitled to say.</span></a>
          </li>
          <li class="toc__row">
            <a href="/data"><span class="toc__n">04</span><span class="toc__t">Data</span>
            <span class="toc__d">Every catchment-month in the study, as a table. {num(OVERALL.catchments * OVERALL.months)} rows.</span></a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Citation</p>
          <p class="margin__v">Stell (2026), <em>The Monitoring Gap</em>. Findings are numbered and stable; figures are numbered within the report.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc { border-top: 1.5px solid var(--ink); }
  .toc__row { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .toc__row a { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s2) var(--s4);
    padding-block: var(--s4); text-decoration: none; transition: background-color 140ms ease; }
  .toc__row a:hover { background: var(--paper-2); }
  .toc__n { font-family: var(--sans); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
    font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--spot); padding-top: 3px; }
  .toc__t { font-size: var(--t-stand); font-weight: 600; }
  .toc__d { grid-column: 2; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: var(--t-note); color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 56ch; }
</style>
